    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The British Crown took over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/company-of-merchants-trading-to-africa">African Company of Merchants</a>' nine forts in 1821; the Bond of 1844, by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fante">Fante</a> and other chiefs submitted murder and robbery to British jurisdiction, laid the legal basis of the colony proclaimed on 24 July 1874.</li><li>British rule was imposed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> by war: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a> was burnt in 1874, occupied again in 1896 when the asantehene was exiled, and annexed as a colony in 1902 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-golden-stool">War of the Golden Stool</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">Cacao</a> trees introduced in 1878 became the colony's mainstay; by the end of the 1940s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> exported more than half of the world's cocoa, grown largely by African farmers.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a> won his seat in the Legislative Assembly election of February 1951 while in jail for sedition; the governor, Sir Charles Arden-Clarke, released him and invited him to form a government as 'leader of government business'.</p></aside>
    </section>

