    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast-castle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cape-coast-castle.jpg" alt="Cape Coast Castle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast-castle">Cape Coast Castle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cape_Coast_Castle,_Cape_Coast,_Ghana.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rjruiziii, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the early nineteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a>, the most powerful state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> interior, pressed southward to protect its trade: it invaded the coast in 1807, fought the Ga-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fante">Fante</a> War of 1811 and the Ashanti–Akim–Akwapim War of 1814–16, and in 1817 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/company-of-merchants-trading-to-africa">African Company of Merchants</a> recognised its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over large areas of the coast. The company held nine trading posts, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast-castle">Cape Coast Castle</a> as its administrative centre. The Fante and the Ga of the new town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a> looked to the British for protection, and in 1821 the Crown dissolved the company and gave the forts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maccarthy">Charles MacCarthy</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>, with a mandate to impose peace and end the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. In 1824 he was killed and most of his force destroyed by the Asante, who were in turn defeated on the coast in 1826.</p><p>Control reverted to the merchants in the late 1820s. From 1830 Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-maclean">George Maclean</a>, president of a council of merchants, made peace with Asante in 1831 and held court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast">Cape Coast</a>; in his thirteen years there were no confrontations and trade reportedly trebled. Crown government returned in 1843 under Commander Henry Worsley Hill, who negotiated the Bond of 1844 with Fante and other chiefs: they were to submit murder and robbery to British jurisdiction, and the document became the legal foundation of British colonisation of the coast. The administration was separated from Sierra Leone in 1850.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> had been a major centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> lasted long into British rule. In 1874 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> declared children born to slaves after 1 January 1875 free and banned debt bondage and pawning, but did not enforce the laws, since the economy depended on slave labour; open slave dealing was tolerated until officials began to act in 1911. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> chiefs were assured in 1896 that they could keep their slaves, chattel slavery was formally banned only in 1908, and the ban was not enforced until the 1920s. Gold stayed in African hands until the 1890s, when modern extraction made mining a foreign enterprise: the Ashanti Goldfields Corporation of 1897 gained a concession of about 160 square kilometres, and the European companies and the colonial government took much of the wealth.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">Cacao</a> trees, introduced in 1878, gave the farmers of the interior their first cash crop; cocoa became the mainstay of the economy in the 1920s, its production lay largely with Africans, the Cocoa Marketing Board was created in 1947, and by the end of that decade the colony exported more than half of the world's supply. The Sekondi–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarkwa">Tarkwa</a> railway, begun in 1898, reached the chief commercial centres of the south, and by 1937 there were 9,700 kilometres of roads. Export revenue paid for schools and services: Achimota College, the artificial harbour at Takoradi, and in 1948 the University College.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-golden-stool"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-the-golden-stool.jpg" alt="War of the Golden Stool" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-golden-stool">War of the Golden Stool</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Typical_fight_in_the_forest,_Anglo-Ashanti_War_of_1900_(Battles_of_the_nineteenth_century,_1901).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The colony was made by the Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> wars. The purchase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> in 1872 cost Asante its ally at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elmina">Elmina</a> and its last outlet to the sea, and in 1873 it invaded the coast for the last time. After early successes its army was driven back beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pra-river">Pra River</a>, and Major General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garnet-wolseley">Sir Garnet Wolseley</a> rejected a settlement. In January 1874 2,500 British soldiers with large numbers of African auxiliaries invaded Asante and occupied and burnt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>. The treaty that followed made Asante renounce its claims to the southern territories and keep the road to Kumasi open; its power declined as subject territories broke away. In 1896 another expedition occupied Kumasi, made Asante a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, abolished the office of asantehene and exiled its holder. The Asante rose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-golden-stool">War of the Golden Stool</a> in 1900 and were defeated the next year.</p><p>The Gold Coast Regiment fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> against German forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> and in the long East African campaign of 1914–18, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> Gold Coast troops served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>. Their service broadened the veterans' horizons, and they came home in 1945 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a>, shortages and unemployment, unwilling to accept the humble positions set aside for Africans. Ex-servicemen stood in the forefront of the discontent that broke into rioting and looting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a> and other towns in early 1948.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard.jpg" alt="Frederick Lugard" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard">Frederick Lugard</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LordLugard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elliott &amp; Fry, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> was four jurisdictions under one governor: the Colony proper on the coast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a>, the Northern Territories, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> from 1902, and, from 1919, British Togoland. Asante and the north were ruled by resident commissioners and by the governor's proclamations until 1946. From 1850 an Executive Council of European officials and a Legislative Council assisted the governor; after 1900 three chiefs and three other Africans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast">Cape Coast</a> and Sekondi joined the Legislative Council, all members were appointed until 1925, and official members always outnumbered the unofficial.</p><p>Local rule followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard">Frederick Lugard</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a>, which worked through chiefs; they had ruled by consent and could be destooled by their elders, but under the colonial government they looked to Accra for decisions, and many came to see themselves as an aristocracy rewarded with honours and knighthoods. Provincial councils of chiefs were set up in 1925, the Native Administration Ordinance of 1927 defined their powers, the Native Authorities Ordinance of 1935 joined central and local government, and the Native Treasuries Ordinance of 1939 first allowed local budgets. The Burns constitution of 1946 gave the Legislative Council eighteen elected members and seats for Asante; the constitution of 1951 added an assembly and African ministers, a prime minister followed in 1952, and in 1954 all members were directly elected.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah.jpg" alt="Kwame Nkrumah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kwame_Nkrumah_on_a_Soviet_stamp_(80th_anniversary_of_his_birth)_1989_CPA_6101.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">USSR Post, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>British rule remade the political map of the region. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a>, which had held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over much of the coast in 1817, was conquered, its king exiled and its federation dismantled; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fante">Fante</a> and other coastal states, who had sought British protection against it, found the Bond of 1844 set aside in 1874 when their land was annexed without consultation. The borders of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> were completed in May 1956, when the people of British Mandated Togoland voted in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> plebiscite to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>. Cocoa, railways, roads and an educational system described as more advanced than any other in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> were built on export revenue, and it was through British-style education that a new elite found the means and the will to seek independence.</p><p>That elite made the colony the centre of West African nationalism. The Aborigines' Rights Protection Society of the 1890s fought a land bill; in 1920 Joseph E. Casely-Hayford convened the National Congress of British West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, the first political solidarity of intellectuals across the British colonies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a>, who had attended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-pan-african-congress">Fifth Pan-African Congress</a> in 1945, returned in 1947 and in 1949 founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-peoples-party">Convention People's Party</a> on a mass following of workers, farmers, ex-servicemen, teachers and market women. The Gold Coast's elected majority of 1946 was called a level of political maturity unequalled in colonial Africa.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The war's end brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a>, shortages and unemployment. Rioting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a> and other towns in early 1948 made African power a British priority. The United <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> Convention, founded in August 1947 by J. B. Danquah, Paa Grant and others, demanded self-government 'in the shortest possible time' and the replacement of chiefs on the Legislative Council by educated men; its general secretary Nkrumah, detained in March 1948 with the 'Big Six', broke away in June 1949 to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-peoples-party">Convention People's Party</a>, which asked for 'self-government now'. The Coussey Committee's constitution of 1951 gave the Executive Council a majority of African ministers, and in February 1951 the CPP won two-thirds of the 104 seats with Nkrumah in jail; Sir Charles Arden-Clarke freed him to lead the government.</p><p>Nkrumah became prime minister in 1952, and the constitution of 1954 left only defence and foreign policy with the governor. The Asante-based National Liberation Movement and the Northern People's Party demanded a federal constitution and accused the CPP of dictatorial tendencies; when they walked out of talks the governor dissolved the assembly, and the Crown promised independence if two-thirds of the new legislature asked for it. In July 1956 the CPP won 57 per cent of the vote and, with the opposition divided, a two-thirds majority; British Togoland had voted that May to join. On 6 March 1957 the Colony of the Gold Coast became independent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> Colony grew from the British forts taken over in 1821 into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-colony">Crown colony</a> that conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a>, annexed the Northern Territories and absorbed British Togoland. Its cocoa, gold and schools bred a nationalist movement led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a>, and on 6 March 1957 it became independent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>.</em></p>

